            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "multi_filter runs several smoothing filters on the same signal at once, \neach at a different degree, and hands you all the results together.\n\nEach is the same one-pole filter as the filter node - move part of \nthe way towards the new value each time - just several of them side by side. \nYou give the degrees as arguments, and you get back a NumPy array with one \nelement per filter.\n\nIn the demo, one sine wave goes through three filters - 0.5, 0.9 and 0.99 - and\nthe plot draws all three: the quicker the filter, the closer it follows the wave;\nthe slower, the smaller and later its copy.\n\nSYNTAX:\nmulti_filter <degree> <degree> ...\n\nEXAMPLE:\nmulti_filter 0.5 0.9 0.99\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe value to filter. Receiving data here triggers the node. \nA single number: multi_filter spreads one signal across many filters, \nit does not filter an array element by element.\n\nfilter 0, filter 1, ...:\nOne inlet per filter, holding that filter's degree, from 0.0 to 1.0 - \nthe same meaning as on the filter node. Higher is smoother and laggier. \nGiving them in increasing order keeps the output in a sensible fast-to-slow\nsequence.\n\nMESSAGES:\n\nset <value> <value> ...\nForces the filters' internal values, one per filter.\n\nclear\nSets every filter back to zero.\n\nOUTPUTS: \n\nout:\nA NumPy array of the smoothed values, one per degree, in the order given.\n\nSEE ALSO:\ndiff_filter    the GAPS between neighbouring smoothings: how much the signal\n               is moving at each timescale\nfilter         one smoothing",
                    "value_type": "string"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 648,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 720,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "string"
                }
            },
            "text": "multi_filter runs several smoothing filters on the same signal at once, \neach at a different degree, and hands you all the results together.\n\nEach is the same one-pole filter as the filter node - move part of \nthe way towards the new value each time - just several of them side by side. \nYou give the degrees as arguments, and you get back a NumPy array with one \nelement per filter.\n\nIn the demo, one sine wave goes through three filters - 0.5, 0.9 and 0.99 - and\nthe plot draws all three: the quicker the filter, the closer it follows the wave;\nthe slower, the smaller and later its copy.\n\nSYNTAX:\nmulti_filter <degree> <degree> ...\n\nEXAMPLE:\nmulti_filter 0.5 0.9 0.99\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe value to filter. Receiving data here triggers the node. \nA single number: multi_filter spreads one signal across many filters, \nit does not filter an array element by element.\n\nfilter 0, filter 1, ...:\nOne inlet per filter, holding that filter's degree, from 0.0 to 1.0 - \nthe same meaning as on the filter node. Higher is smoother and laggier. \nGiving them in increasing order keeps the output in a sensible fast-to-slow\nsequence.\n\nMESSAGES:\n\nset <value> <value> ...\nForces the filters' internal values, one per filter.\n\nclear\nSets every filter back to zero.\n\nOUTPUTS: \n\nout:\nA NumPy array of the smoothed values, one per degree, in the order given.\n\nSEE ALSO:\ndiff_filter    the GAPS between neighbouring smoothings: how much the signal\n               is moving at each timescale\nfilter         one smoothing"
        },
